{"name":"Liquid Blue","hex":"#55b7ce","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"liquid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A substance that is flowing, and keeping no shape, such as water; a substance of which the molecules, while not tending to separate from one another like those of a gas, readily change their relative position, and which therefore retains no definite shape, except that determined by the containing receptacle; an inelastic fluid.","Any of a class of consonant sounds that includes l and r."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Flowing freely like water; fluid; not solid and not gaseous; composed of particles that move freely among each other on the slightest pressure.","Easily sold or disposed of without losing value."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/liquid"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Liquid Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid Blue is an American indie pop rock band/cover band formed in San Diego, California, in 1996 by Scott Stephens and Michael Vangerov. The group has performed in more than 500 cities in 100 countries on six continents and have been recognized as \"the world's most traveled band\". They are more popular in parts of Asia and Europe than in their home country. In 2010 the group scored their first hit in the United States with the song \"Earth Passport\", which reached No. 3 on the Billboard Hot Dance Chart in October 2010. Earth Passport was recorded using nine languages including all six of the official languages of the United Nations. Liquid Blue received a Guinness World Record for \"Earth Passport\" for \"Song Sung in the Most Languages\". In 2011 the group was named \"Entertainer of the Year - Ensemble\" at the Event Solutions Spotlight Awards held in Las Vegas on February 28. The band was \"certified green\" by the County of San Diego in May 2009. The band's official slogan is \"Music To Move You.\" The core unit of Stephens, Vangerov, and Nikki Green have been together since 1998."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Liquid_Blue_Band_Photo.jpg/330px-Liquid_Blue_Band_Photo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid_Blue","wikidata":"Q13643775"},{"title":"Liquid","blocks":[{"text":"Liquid is a state of matter with a definite volume but no fixed shape. When confined in a container and subject to a force such as gravity, liquids will adapt to the internal shape of the container in the direction of the force. Liquids are nearly incompressible, maintaining their volume even under pressure. The density of a liquid is usually close to that of a solid, and much higher than that of a gas. Liquids are a form of condensed matter alongside solids, and a form of fluid alongside gases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Water_drop_001.jpg/330px-Water_drop_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquid","wikidata":"Q11435"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Liquid_Blue_Belarus.jpg/500px-Liquid_Blue_Belarus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Liquid_Blue_Belarus.jpg/1280px-Liquid_Blue_Belarus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Liquid_Blue_Belarus.jpg/500px-Liquid_Blue_Belarus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Liquid Blue in Belarus, 2008","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALiquid_Blue_Belarus.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Liquid_Blue_Beijing.jpg/500px-Liquid_Blue_Beijing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Liquid_Blue_Beijing.jpg/1280px-Liquid_Blue_Beijing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Liquid_Blue_Beijing.jpg/500px-Liquid_Blue_Beijing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Liquid Blue in Beijing, China, 2007","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALiquid_Blue_Beijing.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Cavitating-prop.jpg/500px-Cavitating-prop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Cavitating-prop.jpg/500px-Cavitating-prop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cavitation in water from a boat propeller","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACavitating-prop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/500px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/1280px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg/500px-2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface waves in water","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2006-01-14_Surface_waves.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Viscosities.gif/500px-Viscosities.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Viscosities.gif/500px-Viscosities.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A simulation of viscosity. The fluid on the left has a lower viscosity and Newtonian behavior while the liquid on the right has higher viscosity and non-Newtonian behavior.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AViscosities.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG/500px-Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG/1280px-Blue_Lava_lamp.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm